\chapter{USB: Keyboard and Mouse}
\label{ch:usb}

With a screen on the HDMI port ({\it chapter \ref{ch:video}}) the machine also needs a keyboard that does not hang on a serial line. On the Raspberry Pi 4B the USB sockets are
connected in a roundabout way: a chip (the VIA VL805) sits on the PCI Express bus and offers four ports. To use them the kernel needs three layers:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{PCI Express} ([[pci.c]]): the bus on which the USB chip is found. Every device on it has a 4096 byte \emph{configuration space} with its vendor, its class and its
  \emph{base address registers} (BARs): the device says how much address space it needs, the kernel chooses where it goes. The code here is for the \emph{generic} PCI host (qemu's [[virt]] machine,
  ``ECAM'': the configuration space of device $d$ is a piece of memory); the Raspberry Pi's own controller is the second part of this chapter.
\item \textbf{xHCI} ([[xhci.c]]): the \emph{host controller} for USB 3. Software and controller talk through \emph{rings} in RAM, circular lists of 16 byte entries (\emph{transfer request blocks}, TRBs): the
  driver writes commands and transfers into rings and rings a doorbell, the controller writes the results into the event ring. Each side marks the entries that are new with a \emph{cycle bit}, which is
  flipped on every round.
  The driver enumerates the devices (reset the port, get an address, read the descriptors) and starts the HID interrupt transfers.
\item \textbf{HID} ([[usbhid.c]]): keyboards and mice speak the \emph{boot protocol}: a keyboard sends 8 bytes (modifier keys, up to six keys that are pressed now), a mouse 3 or 4 bytes (buttons, movement).
  A key press becomes a character (US or German layout) or an escape sequence for the cursor keys, and goes the way of a character from the serial line ([[arch_console_input]]).
\end{enumerate}

The driver polls: the timer calls [[usb_tick]] a hundred times a second, which reads the event ring. No interrupt handler is needed for a keyboard, and it keeps the code short.

Not done: hubs (a keyboard must sit directly on a port of the controller), isochronous and bulk transfers (USB sticks, audio), the LEDs of the keyboard, interrupts.

