\section{PCI Express, the generic host (see \texttt{pci.c})}
\label{src:pci.c}

The device tree tells where the configuration space is and which address window the devices may use ([[plat.pci_cfg]], [[plat.pci_mem_pci]], [[plat.pci_mem_cpu]], [[plat.pci_mem_size]]: the window as the devices
see it and as the cores see it; they are the same on qemu). Device $d$ of bus $b$, function $f$ has its registers at [[cfg + (b << 20 | d << 15 | f << 12)]]. The first 64 bytes are the same for all devices:
vendor and device (offset 0), command (4: bit 1 memory space on, bit 2 bus master on), class code (8, the upper 24 bits: base class, sub class, interface), header type (0x0c), the six BARs (0x10 \ldots).

To find the size of a BAR the kernel writes all ones into it and reads it back: the bits that the device does not implement stay zero, so the size is the lowest bit that is set. Bits 1 and 2 say whether
the BAR is memory (bit 0 clear) and whether it is 64 bits wide (it then uses the next BAR for the upper half). A BAR must be put on an address that is a multiple of its size.

