\section{The xHCI host controller (see \texttt{xhci.c})}
\label{src:xhci.c}

The controller has three groups of registers behind BAR 0. The \emph{capability registers} (read only: number of ports and slots, the offsets of the others), the \emph{operational registers} (command and status, the
pointers to the rings, one set of port registers per root port) and the \emph{runtime registers} (the event ring: where it is and how far we have read). A fourth group, the \emph{doorbells}, is an array of
registers: writing the number of a slot (0: the controller itself) and of an endpoint tells the controller that new work is in a ring.

Everything the controller reads or writes lives in RAM at addresses that the \emph{controller} can use: on our boards the physical addresses ([[PA]]). The structures are static, aligned arrays of the kernel;
because the controller does not look at the caches of the cores (on the Pi it does not), every structure that we write is cleaned out of the cache before the controller reads it ([[cache_clean]]) and
every one that the controller wrote is invalidated before we read it ([[cache_flush_inval]]); on qemu that does nothing wrong.

\paragraph{Rings.} A ring is an array of TRBs; the last one is a \emph{link} that points back to the start. The producer owns a \emph{cycle bit}: it writes it into the TRBs it makes; the
consumer treats a TRB as new if its cycle bit equals the one it expects. When the producer passes the link TRB it flips its bit (and the link TRB has the ``toggle cycle'' flag, so the consumer flips, too).
There are three kinds: the \emph{command ring} (we produce commands, the controller consumes), the \emph{event ring} (the controller produces, we consume; it is described by a table of segments) and one \emph{transfer ring} per endpoint.

